# INTRO(3)

## NAME

intro - introduction to library functions

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

#include <math.h>
```

## DESCRIPTION

This section describes functions that may be found in various libraries, other than those
functions that directly invoke UNIX system primitives, which are described in section 2.
Functions are divided into various libraries distinguished by the section number at the top of
the page:

(3)
: These functions, together with those of section 2 and those marked (3S), constitute library
  *libc,* which is automatically loaded by the C compiler b6cc(1). The link editor b6ld(1)
  searches this library under the `-lc` option. Declarations for some of these functions may be
  obtained from include files indicated on the appropriate pages. **Note:** there is no Fortran
  compiler here.

(3M)
: These functions constitute the math library, *libm.* The link editor searches this library
  under the `-lm` option. Declarations for these functions may be obtained from the include file
  <math.h>. **Note:** its pages live in lib/libm, not here.

(3S)
: These functions constitute the `standard I/O package`, see stdio(3). These functions are in the
  library *libc* already mentioned. Declarations for these functions may be obtained from the
  include file <stdio.h>.

(3X)
: Various specialized libraries have not been given distinctive captions. The files in which
  these libraries are found are named on the appropriate pages. **Note:** the two that exist here
  are *libtermcap* and *libcurses*, whose pages live in lib/libtermcap and lib/libcurses.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| share/besm6/lib/libm.a, libtermcap.a, libcurses.a
| share/besm6/lib/crt0.o, libruntime.a

## SEE ALSO

stdio(3), b6nm(1), b6ld(1), b6cc(1), intro(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Functions in the math library (3M) may return conventional values when the function is
undefined for the given arguments or when the value is not representable. In these cases the
external variable *errno* (see intro(2)) is set to the value EDOM or ERANGE. **Note:** EDOM and
ERANGE are defined in <errno.h>, not in <math.h> as v7's page said.

**Note:** "when the value is not representable" is sharper here than it was on the PDP-11. This
machine has *no infinities and no NaNs,* so a routine that would have returned one returns
HUGE_VAL - the largest *finite* value, and not a distinguished one. And an exponent that
overflows 2**63 is an arithmetic *fault* that ends the program, so every range check in libm is
made *before* the arithmetic rather than after it. See exp(3M).

## ASSEMBLER

v7's page gave a PDP-11 calling sequence here. The convention on this machine is in
doc/Besm6_Calling_Conventions.md: arguments are pushed in direct order below r15, the *last*
one is left in the accumulator, r14 holds the negative argument count, r13 the return address,
and the result comes back in the accumulator.

**Note:** one consequence shows up on several pages in section 2. A system-call stub has *no
prologue and does not pop the stack,* because the C convention has already put the arguments
where the gate reads them and the gate performs the callee's cleanup itself. That is why
lib/libc/sys is assembly and not C.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### Link order is a contract

> ```
> crt0.o objects... [-lm] [-lcurses] [-ltermcap] -lc -lruntime
> ```

*B6ld* scans each archive exactly once, where it stands, and pulls a member only for a symbol
still undefined. libm calls into libc, libcurses calls into libtermcap and libc, libtermcap
calls into libc, libc calls the compiler-support helpers of libruntime, and **none of them
calls back.**

*Libruntime.a* comes from the external c-compiler and supplies nothing but those helpers. The
libc, the crt0.o and the headers are this repository's.

### One hazard is silent

A routine missing from libc.a can be satisfied from libruntime.a instead of failing to link.
Relink without it and read the errors - every undefined name must be a **b$\***:

> ```
> b6ld crt0.o prog.o -o /dev/null -L.../libc -lc
> ```
